    51	"Every sheet," she said. "Every perimeter. Every practitioner the office ever sent to a line with a sigil, and every one of ours, and Teague's four at the Hall in the autumn, and the seven on the shelf yesterday who wouldn't step over a stake. It's the same. Not the same *kind* of thing — the *same*. The same stride. The same instant. A Silver goes at the line at the stride a Copper goes at. An Iron goes where a Tide goes. A man forty years on a Path goes where a boy a month on one goes, and neither of them goes a hair before the other, and neither of them a hair after, and it doesn't matter who they are, or what they've done, or how they were taught, or whether they believe in it." She put her good hand flat on the column. "I've got it on more than twenty sheets in an office's hand and on every page of my own since the Ring and on four floors of walls that say nothing about it at all, and I said it once at a spring and stopped, and I'm not stopping now, because a finding you stop three times is a finding you're afraid of, and I'm not afraid of it. I'm afraid of what comes after it, and that's a different thing."
