     3	He logged the leg as a leg, in five entries, one a night, because the road was the road and the sites were not, and he wanted the two kept apart on the page.
     4	
     5	*Two hundred and ninety-ninth.* East from the ford on the bearing, twelve and a mule, the Stair road under them to the Stair's bowl by the last of the light — the rim, the floor below it with its terraces going down, the iron a stride apart round it as the registry had set it; not entered; Karis at the rim with the cord for the hour there was, checking a number she already had; camp on the rim. *Three hundredth.* The road to Oryn's last three holds turned north off the bearing at a cairn at noon, and she looked at it once, straight, and walked on east with the roll on the mule, and nobody spoke, because the terms had been said and the day she had to was not this one. The Hall's marsh by dusk the same day; the stone at its edge where he had read himself twice by a fire in the autumn. *Three hundred and first. Three hundred and second.* Past the marsh, on the bearing, three days of ground none of the six had walked and Teague's four had walked once, in the autumn, carrying a healer to a rival's shoulder; two camps with no water. *Three hundred and third.* The crest.
     6	
     7	He entered, under each date, the same line, because it was true under each: *known*. Not followed — Seln had the count at every halt and there was nothing behind — but read, off a sheet, by somebody who had the sheet, and he did not stop entering it, because a feeling that recurs is a fact about the road.
