   255	Then there were no ruts, and the ground rose a little, and Seln was standing on the crown of it.
   256	
   257	The basin lay below them as round as a plate. The reeds in it were green at the foot now, and old straw above; and every one of them stopped at the one edge, on every side, as though a person had gone round with shears. A pale straight path crossed them. Four grey walls stood in the water at the end of it, roofless, with the evening in them.
   258	
   259	He heard the boy's breath go in, behind him.
   260	
   261	The four from the house on the rise had seen that place once, from where they stood now. They had found six pairs of breeches steaming on a rope, and a very big man lying strapped. Teague looked at it a good while with his thumbs in his belt.
   262	
   263	The old man had come up last. He had stood a stride behind Brom on the Stair's brow, the day before, and looked down at a grey floor for the whole of an hour without a word; and Cael had thought then that a person who has read six years of sheets upon a place might have little left to say of the place itself. He stopped now at the rise's crown, on the stick, and his eyes went round the edge of the reeds, slowly, the whole way, as they would have gone round a seal.
   264	
   265	"It is at that edge," he said. "Where they stop."
   266	
   267	"To a boot's width," said Lira. "You could walk it with your eyes shut."
   268	
   269	"I have put my name to a good many papers which said that such a thing could not be. Not one of them was drawn by a person who had stood here." He considered the hall for a breath longer. "I find that I am glad to have been spared the sight until I had left off signing."
   270	
   271	"We'll not go down to the reeds," said Teague. "Nor will the beast. It's the track's end, on the grass, and nobody drinks what stands in that."
   272	
   273	They made their two fires a few yards outside the grey stone.
   274	
   275	It was there still; no spade had been near it. The stake stood a boot's width from it, scaled in leaves of rust, with two figures under its beaten head and nothing beneath them. Cael sat down against it, since his back knew the place.
   276	
   277	He had read himself here twice. It had been night, and he had lain between the embers and this iron with nothing left in him to be frightened with. He had gone to the crowded place under his breastbone and found it where it had always been, with ten things going round it by a way that was worn smooth; and a few old words in pencil had gone onto it, and fitted. He had read it again before light, and told a healer, with his hand on her wrist.
   278	
   279	She had said to write it down and stop touching it.
   280	
   281	He had two findings of that place now. One was hers. It had cost her forty minutes and the floor of herself, at a mill, and it said that there was no flow. The other lay behind his eyes in two words. He had not gone for those, and the tool that gave them had come from a man who said that it was not for men. They did not agree, and he had told nobody.
   282	
   283	The twelfth leaned.
   284	
   285	It had found the iron behind his shoulder: a made thing, driven by a mallet some six cold terms ago. He felt it come about to that, mildly, and he gave his eyes the reeds. There were a great many. He counted the green ones along a yard of the edge until the lean came up short and lay down again.
   286	
   287	His hands were on his knees, and he left them there. A sore place was to be let alone; he had been told so on this very turf, and he had got round it once since by a field's breadth.
   288	
   289	Out in the water, a long way off, something knocked twice against stone.
   290	
   291	Lira's head came up at the fire. So did Brom's.
   292	
   293	"It's alive," she said.
   294	
   295	"We left it so," said Cael.
   296	
   297	He took the book out, and wrote by the last of the light.
   298	
   299	*Three hundredth day. The track's end, outside the stone, at the hall's marsh. A hundred and twenty-three: four at a gate, for two skins, meal, a cheese, and corn. Vastin: twenty-one.*
   300	
   301	*The cairn at noon. Her road went north there. She looked up it once, and walked on east. Brom's shoulder has until the three hundred and fifth; that is hers to count, and she has counted it. I did not ask.*
   302	
   303	*Known: the keeper had our skins filled before we came. A man on a bay told him two days ago that a crew would want them.*
   304	
   305	*I am sitting where I read it twice. I have not touched it.*
   306	
   307	*Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*

