     1	# Chapter 48 — Never Below
     2	
     3	They struck the east road an hour from the marsh, on the three hundred and first morning, and it was a different road from the one that Cael remembered.
     4	
     5	He had walked it once under a frost, with every rut ringing and the pools shut. The ruts were dust now. The pale grass had come green from the root, and the day was mild; and in all the ground that he could see from a captain's shoulder there was not a thread of running water. It had struck him at the marsh, and it grew on him as they went. Every beck that came down off those hills ran for a furlong, or for a mile, and then went into a hole in the ground like a rat into a wall. The country drank its own water and kept it.
     6	
     7	A fist went up at the first hold.
     8	
     9	It was Seln's, far ahead, and Teague had been looking at the same thing for some time. There was the fence of split posts, and the long house, and smoke. There was the gate. It was the middle of a fine forenoon, a month and more after the thaw, when a hold has its beasts out and its people in the fields; and that gate was shut, with a bar across it, and not a head showed on the walk.
    10	
    11	They went by. Nobody called to them.
    12	
    13	The second hold lay four miles on, and its gate was barred as well.
    14	
    15	Brom left the file there, at two fingers from the front. He stood under the gate-walk with the mule's empty bucket, and said something up at it; and after a while a rope came down over the posts with a full one on its end, and went up again with a copper. Nobody opened the gate. Nobody put a face over the wall. He brought the bucket back along the road at arm's length, so that it should not slop, and held it under a brown nose.
    16	
    17	"One," he said, very low, when he passed. "For the beast. They'll not sell a second, and they'll not say why."
    18	
    19	The mule drank it to the wood, and looked for another.
    20	
    21	Cael had kept his fist down, both times. A captain could see a barred gate as well as he could; Teague had turned his head toward each for the space of three strides, and had walked on.
    22	
    23	They met no cart all that day. The file went as it had been set, and he listened to it behind him, since his eyes were wanted in front. A new sound had come into it since the ford. The lean old woman's staff and the old man's stick went down together now. Neither of them had chosen that, he thought: she walked beside the pannier, and he a stride behind Brom, ten yards apart; but two pieces of ash that strike the same road all day will find the one beat. Whenever the file stood, he heard the two of them stop on the one tap.
    24	
    25	The bow was the slow one, by the afternoon. That hip had not cared for a marsh's damp, and its owner came on at the tail with his crossbow slung, saying nothing to anybody. Cael heard the boy's feet break their time once, and turn, and heard them come back to it at a sharp small sound, such as a man makes with his tongue to a dog.
    26	
    27	They came to the third hold at dusk.
    28	
    29	It was as dead as it had been. The fence lay outward along one side, and the tree in the cart-yard had come into leaf; the one whole gable stood up twice Brom's height, of turf and stone, with the sky going green behind it. Teague looked at the place as he looked at any ground, from the rim inward. Then he walked through the fallen fence to a ring of stones in the yard's middle, with a rotten windlass leaning over it, and stood looking down.
    30	
    31	Cael went with him, since he had not been told to stop.
    32	
    33	It had been a well. The shaft went down ten feet, neatly steined, and ended in a floor of dry rubble and last year's leaves.
    34	
    35	"That's what kills them, out here," said Teague. It was the first thing that he had said aloud since the marsh. "Nobody burned this, and nobody starved in it. The water went down a rod one dry year, and they dug. It went down another." He turned from it. "A hold can lose its beasts and come again. It can't lose that."
    36	
    37	"And the two back there?"
    38	
    39	"They've a rod of rope more than they'd like, I'd say. That's why it was a bucket." He put his thumbs in his belt. "It's not why it was a bar."
    40	
    41	---
    42	
    43	They made their fires in the lee of the gable, and it was a dry camp.
    44	
    45	Brom told off the water before anybody had sat down: there were ten skins between the two crews, and he had hefted each. He gave it as a cup apiece that night and a cup at first light, with the kettle for the old woman's linen and whatever was left for the mule; and he said that he would hear no argument, since a beast which carried three boxes had a better claim than any of them.
    46	
    47	"She's had a bucket," said the boy, across the gap.
    48	
    49	"She's had one bucket, and she's four times your weight, and she's never once asked me what was for supper." Brom did not look round. "I'll thank you to leave my mule out of it."
    50	
    51	"It's her mule," said Lira.
    52	
    53	"It was a manner of speaking."
    54	
    55	The healer, who was sitting on a fallen post with the rope under her boot, said nothing at all; but Cael saw the corner of her mouth go down.
    56	
