    58	
    59	He broke it across his knee himself.
    60	
    61	"I've been wanting to do that for a hundred and eighty days," he said, to the two halves.
    62	
    63	Cael drank his cup slowly. He had his back to the turf of the gable, and the book shut on his knee, and he had not yet written, when a pair of boots came across the gap between the fires and stopped.
    64	
    65	Teague bent his knees, and sat down on the grass beside him.
    66	
    67	He did it as he had done it once before, at a marsh's edge, with his forearms on his knees and his head no higher than a boy's. The right wrist lay along his thigh in its leather, a foot from Cael's hand; and he looked at the fire, and let a little time go by.
    68	
    69	Cael did not wait to be asked a thing, this time.
    70	
    71	"You'd seen both gates."
    72	
    73	"I had."
    74	
    75	"I'd seen them too. I kept my fist down, since you'd turned your head."
    76	
    77	"I know that you did. I was waiting for it." Teague picked a stem of grass. "That's what I came over to say, and I'd sooner say it sitting. I didn't put you at my shoulder to tell me what I've missed. A man who's only told what he's missed never learns what the other pair of eyes is worth. Tell me what I've seen as well. Then I know that there's two of us looking, and which way the second one's turned."
    78	
    79	"That's a great many fists."
    80	
    81	"It's fewer than you'd think; there's three things worth one." He broke the stem, and laid down a piece for each. "A thing that's out of its place. A thing that's stopped, and ought to be moving. A thing that's moving, and ought to be stopped. The rest is country."
    82	
    83	Cael went over the day by that rule, as he would have gone over a column. A hare was none of the three; a gate that was barred at noon, in a thaw, was the second, and he had let it go by twice.
    84	
    85	"The gates were shut against us," he said. "Not against the road. A hold that's afraid of a road keeps a head on its walk to see what comes down it. There was none on either."
    86	
    87	"So?"
    88	
    89	"So they'd been told what was coming, and that they'd do well not to have looked at it." He heard how it came out. "That's a man on a bay again."
    90	
    91	"That's what I made it." The grey eyes had not left the fire. "I'd not have had the bay; I never heard of him till that big one spoke at the cairn. You had him, and you had the gates, and you put the two together sitting on your hands. I'd have liked it at noon."
    92	
    93	"Noted."
    94	
    95	Teague glanced at him, sideways, as a man glances at a tool that he has heard a good deal about.
    96	
    97	"That's a short word for a boy who writes so much," he said. He got up in one piece, without a hand to the ground, and went back across the gap.
    98	
    99	*Three hundred and first day. A dead hold on the east road, in the lee of its gable. A hundred and twenty-three. Vastin: twenty-two. Oryn is a day past her cairn. A cup of water a head, and the kettle.*
   100	
   101	*Three things are worth a fist: what is out of its place; what is stopped and ought to move; what moves and ought to be stopped. I am to give it for what he has seen as well as for what he has not.*
   102	
   103	*Known: two holds with their gates barred at noon and nobody on the walk. They were told. One bucket, over a wall, for the mule.*
   104	
   105	*Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
   106	
   107	---
   108	
   109	They came to the heap of chock-stones two hours after sunrise, and he knew it before the hand went up.
   110	
   111	The road divided there. The right hand went on south and east under the foot of a long bare ridge. The left climbed away toward a notch between two hills. There was no frost in the notch that morning; it stood up green, with a white cloud sitting in it.
   112	
   113	He had walked fifty paces up that left-hand road, once, at a healer's stride and a half, and had asked her nothing the whole way. She had stopped him. She had said where she was to be found, and what he was to bring. He had turned round to four people and a mule.
   114	
   115	There were eleven now, and two beasts' worth of load on one.
   116	
   117	He did not look round for her. He heard the pannier creak, and the four hooves go by the stones without a check; and when the file bent with the road he saw her at the edge of his eye, with the rope over her shoulder and her face to the front. She had been a day and a half past her own turning when she came to that heap. She did not give the notch so much as her chin.
   118	
   119	The road swung south before noon, and the line did not.
   120	
   121	Seln stood at the bend with his arm out, upward. They left the ruts there and climbed, with Brom on the low side of the mule; and inside the hour they were on the back of the ridge.
   122	
   123	It was a great whale's back of grass, a mile broad on its crown, with the country falling off on either hand. He had seen it under the clearest sky of his life, with a white stroke of frost in every fold. The folds were green now, and the sky was a soft high grey, and it was warm. That was the trouble with it. Twelve people and a loaded beast had been walking since the marsh on a cup at night and a cup at morning, and by the noon halt the boy's lips had a line of white along them.
   124	
   125	Brom hefted the skins again. He did not say a figure. He held up one of them to Teague by its neck, and let it hang, and it hung like an empty sleeve.
   126	
   127	"There's none on the top," said Teague. "There never is, on a back like this; it sheds to both sides and keeps nothing." His eyes were on the southern flank, where the ridge went down in great green scoops. "It comes out somewhere. Half way down, as a rule, where the rock changes. I want the folds looked into, every one, and I want it done from the edge."
   128	
   129	He sent Seln along the southern lip, and the boy with him, for his legs.
   130	
   131	It was the middle of the afternoon when the fist went up.
   132	
   133	It went up a long way ahead, small against the grey, and it stayed up; and then the other hand rose beside it and was turned over, empty, and turned back. Cael had never seen those two signs made together. He gave them to the shoulder in front of him by saying so, low, and was told that he need not; a man could see that for himself, and it meant *something, and I don't know what to call it*.
   134	
   135	They came up to the place in file.
   136	
   137	The fold went down from their boots in a long hollow, like the inside of a scoop. Half way down it a band of dark rock ran right across, as tall as a house wall and split in leaves; above the band the grass was pale, and below it there was a slope of broken shale with turf in patches. Under the band's western end the stone was black with wet.
   138	
   139	He saw that first, since he had been looking for nothing else since the morning. Then he saw what stood about it.
   140	
   141	They were all down that slope, and along the foot of the band. He counted nineteen grey backs before he had drawn two breaths. Each of them was bigger than a cart, and plated like a slate roof, and they stood with their horn beaks to the rock.
   142	
   143	"Shale-backs," said Brom, at his elbow, in a voice with no breath in it. "On a hill. Over the only water in three days."
   144	
   145	---
   146	
   147	Nobody went down.
   148	
   149	Teague stood on the lip with his thumbs in his belt, and he looked for a great deal longer than Cael had ever seen him look at anything. The file had come up and gone to ground behind him without a hand given; and the mule was held a hundred yards back from the edge, with her head turned away and Brom's coat over her eyes, since she had smelled the wet.
   150	
   151	"I'll say what that is, for the ones who've not worked under them," he said at last. He spoke low, and to the fold. "They're as mild as milch beasts. A salt string walks under a herd on the cold road twice in a year, and it's let alone. There's a rule, and it's two halves. Never be below them. Never be loud below them."
   152	
   153	"It's below them," said Lira. "The water. All of it."
   154	
   155	"It is."
   156	
   157	"We have a leaf upon them," said Karis. She had it out, and she read from it as she would have read a measure. "They do not answer fire, or a noise, or a blow struck on their own rock. They answer fright in one of their own kind, all together and at once; and by that and the carters' word they will answer a sigil in the same fashion. It is derived. I have never seen it tested, and I am thankful."
   158	
   159	"I've seen it," said Teague. "Not with my eyes. I've stood where it was done." He did not turn his head. "A block came down on the cold road, in the one-armed man's time, under a ledge herd; and a man at the tail of the string shifted it as I'd shift a cart. They found two mules. He was a Force. I've thought of him each time that I've put my hand to a thing since."
   160	
   161	Nobody on that lip said anything.
   162	
   163	"So there's no Path opened on this hill, by anybody, for any cause. I'd have said so without her leaf. Now look at them, and tell me what they're doing."
   164	
   165	It was said to the shoulder behind him, and Cael looked.
   166	
   167	They were not grazing; he could see that much. Seven or eight stood at the wet, with their beaks to the black rock, and the rest were strung away eastward along the foot of the band, a length and a half apart, with their heads down in the lichen.
   168	
   169	"They lick it. The ones at the wet."
   170	
   171	"At first light, and again at dusk. Between, they mow along the band away from it, and back." Teague lifted his chin toward the far end of the row. "It's the middle of the afternoon. Which way are they going?"
   172	
   173	"The far one's turned," said Lira.
   174	
   175	She was on her knees at the lip, with her chin up; she had read feet for longer than she had had a rank, and she had read a herd once from a sledge, with one arm tied.

