   257	It was Brom who came, and Cael had known that it would be: the man was slow to answer, and hard to hurry, and no shale-back in that fold could have walked a hillside more like one of its own. He arrived at the ledge at last, breathing through his nose, and looked where a hand was laid.
   258	
   259	"That leaf. It's loose at the foot. There's a bucket behind it."
   260	
   261	"How d'you—" Brom stopped. He had glanced up the slope, to where a captain stood on a lip. "No. I'll ask you at a fire."
   262	
   263	He put both hands to the stone.
   264	
   265	They went to it at the height of his shoulders, which they could not have reached five days before. He did not wrench it; he rocked it, twice, as he would have rocked a post in its hole, and then drew it toward him, and a slab of shale as long as a man's arm came away from the face and was laid down upon the ledge without a sound.
   266	
   267	The water came out behind it in a rope as thick as a thumb.
   268	
   269	The tall man had been kneeling a yard off, with a skin's neck under the old drip. He looked at that rope of water, and then at Cael, with his pale eyes, for the length of a breath. Then he shifted the skin beneath it; and his free hand lifted a little from his knee, and came down again upon it, once.
   270	
   271	They filled eight skins under a herd, and Cael tied none of them, since he could not see the thongs.
   272	
   273	"She's coming," said Lira, when the seventh was at the water. "Along the top of the band. Don't look up."
   274	
   275	"How near?"
   276	
   277	"She's over us."
   278	
   279	Something dripped on the back of his neck that was not the seam's. He heard the rasp begin, a man's height above his head, slow and enormous, and a little grit came down into the pan.
   280	
   281	"She's licking the top of it," said Lira, in the same voice. "She wants the wet; she doesn't want you. Finish that one. Then it's three steps west before anybody stands up, and we go out as we came."
   282	
   283	A calf came down to look at Brom while the last skin filled. It was the size of a small ox, and it set its beak against the toe of his boot, and scraped, twice. He stood through that like a boulder, until it had found nothing that it cared for, and went away.
   284	
   285	"It took me for a stone," he said, on the lip, when they were all over it.
   286	
   287	"It's not the first," said Lira.
   288	
   289	---
   290	
   291	They slept on the ridge's back that night, in a hollow, over two small fires of what Brom had carried up from a dead hold's yard, and it was the second dry camp; but there were ten full skins in it.
   292	
   293	The mule drank four.
   294	
   295	Oryn had unrolled her strip of canvas in a dip of the turf, and pressed it down with her boot until it would hold; and Brom emptied the skins into that one after another, with a face like a man paying a debt in his own coin. The brown head went down, and it did not come up until the canvas showed.
   296	
   297	"There," he said to her. "And you'll never know where it came from. You're the only one of us who's had a quiet day."
   298	
   299	"She smelled it for an hour with your coat on her head," said Lira. "I'd not call that quiet."
   300	
   301	So four more had been wanted, and they had been fetched before the light went. The tall man took the first pair, and the boy took the second. Nobody had told him that he might; a captain had only looked at two skins lying on the grass, and then at him. He went down that slope at three steps and a stand, with nineteen grey backs above him and the whole herd gathered to the wet. Cael, who could see a furlong well enough and nothing nearer, watched a small dark shape take as long over sixty yards as it had ever taken over anything in its life.
   302	
   303	He came back over the lip with both skins full, and nobody said a word to him. But the bow, sitting on the turf with his leg out, reached up as he went by and took one of them out of his hand; and the boy, who was all wrists, stood for a breath looking at the hand that was left empty.
   304	
   305	Seln had gone down last of all, alone, when the beasts had left the pan for the night's lying.
   306	
   307	He laid what he had found on the grass between the two fires, since it was for both: an iron hoop, as wide as a man's two hands, with a rivet in it.
   308	
   309	"That is off a keg. It lay in the shale under the ledge, and it has not been there for a cold term; there is no scale on it." He set a finger beside it, and did not touch it. "There are boot-nails in the mud of the pan, under the beasts' treading. They are a made boot's, in rows, and I have five different heels. The newest is a week old."
   310	
   311	"Carters," said Brom.
   312	
   313	"No cart could come within two miles of that fold. Somebody walks to it, with kegs on a beast, as often as a keg is emptied." He looked east along the ridge, where there was nothing to see but grass going grey. "It is a day from here to the brow. I think that a posting sends for its water."
   314	
   315	Teague had come across the gap to look at the hoop, and he stood over it with his thumbs in his belt.
   316	
   317	"Then we'll see their fire tomorrow," he said.
   318	
   319	His near sight was coming back, slowly, as it had after a calf and a river. He held the book at the length of his arm to write in it, and the pencil's point was the last thing to come clear.
   320	
   321	*Three hundred and second day. The ridge's back, in a hollow. A hundred and twenty-three. Vastin: twenty-three. Oryn is two days past her cairn; she went by the chock-stones and did not look up.*
   322	
   323	*Water, at a seep under a band of shale, with a herd on the slope. Teague read it: the hour, the old cow, the way in. I would not have had the half. The tall one stopped the boy with a hand.*
   324	
   325	*The read: the ledge, for six breaths. A bucket behind a loose leaf. I wanted Pressure and went without. Brom drew the leaf with both arms. The eyes, for an hour.*
   326	
   327	*Thirty-two, and that one. Deployment since the Line: thirty-three.*
   328	
   329	*Known: a keg's hoop at the pan, and five made heels. Somebody is watered from that fold.*

