     1	# Chapter 49 — Seven on a Posting
     2	
     3	The three hundred and third day was the clearest that they had had since the ford, and Cael walked the whole of it toward a thing that he had already seen.
     4	
     5	He had come along that ridge once before with four people, and had been able to see for thirty miles. There had been no road in any of it, and no roof, and nothing with a seal on; he had taken out a book at noon and written that he loved the country, which he had never written of anywhere. He remembered what had been in the same coat with it. A strip of brass had lain under his feet for three days, and he had been more afraid of where it went than of a thing in a marsh.
     6	
     7	Both were true still. The hills went away blue behind hills, and the wind came over the grass in long grey strokes, and a hawk hung above the southern folds. And at the end of the ridge there was a fence.
     8	
     9	There were twelve on it now. He could hear them behind him as he had not been able to hear four: the pannier, and the hooves, and two pieces of ash going down on the one beat. He found, somewhat to his surprise, that he liked the sound. It was a good deal harder to feel small in front of a thing with eleven pairs of boots at his back.
    10	
    11	Teague called the noon halt in a hollow, out of the wind, where the two crews sat down a length apart as they did at every halt.
    12	
    13	Lira came and sat by him with her bread.
    14	
    15	"He never drank his cup, the night before last," she said. She did not look across the gap. "The boy. I watched it. The kettle had gone to the old one's linen, and there was nothing left to heat; so he put his own cup on the embers, and wrung the cloth out of that, and took it over on his two hands. He went to his blanket dry."
    16	
    17	"Did Teague know?"
    18	
    19	"He held his arm out for it. I'd say that he knew to the drop." She broke the bread. "He let him. I've been turning that over since. I'd have told the boy not to be a fool, and I'd have been wrong; it's the one thing in that crew that's his."
    20	
    21	Cael looked. The boy was sitting with his back to a tussock, all wrists, chewing as though somebody were about to take the bread away. He had walked a slope under nineteen grey backs at dusk, at three steps and a stand, and had come up it with two skins full. Since then he had carried himself a little differently, like a person who has been given a tool and is not yet sure that he is allowed to keep it.
    22	
    23	"He asked me this morning whether he'd walked it right," said Lira.
    24	
    25	"What did you tell him?"
    26	
    27	"That he'd gone down it like a man carrying eggs, and that it was right." Her mouth went a little to one side. "He nodded at me with the whole of his head. I thought it was going to come off."
    28	
    29	There was a stick grounded in the grass on Cael's other hand.
    30	
    31	The old man had not sat. He stood with the stiff leg a little before the other, as he stood at every halt now, since getting down cost him more than staying up; and he was looking east along the ridge with the attention that he gave to a sheet.
    32	
    33	"I am informed that we shall see a fence by dusk," he said.
    34	
    35	"By dusk. Or a little before."
    36	
    37	"Then there is a thing which I should like to have said before we see it, and before anybody is put to the trouble of asking me." He did not turn his head. "I have twenty-four of thirty. A captain has built a count upon what I carry, and I have no doubt that he would find a use for it tomorrow. I shall not stand it. I was told eleven days more, at a mill, by a person who was entitled to tell me; and I broke her word once, under a town, and learned the price."
    38	
    39	"Nobody was going to ask."
    40	
    41	"I did not suppose that anybody was. I have observed that out here a thing is entered before it is wanted." He considered that. "It is a tidier practice than the one that I was trained in. I had rather the refusal stood in your book this noon than that it should have to be made on a hillside, in front of ten people, with a reason."
    42	
    43	"It's entered," said Cael.
    44	
    45	"I am obliged."
    46	
    47	He was silent for a breath, with both hands on the ash.
    48	
    49	"I should add that I do not know what I am for, tomorrow, without it. I have not been upon a field in forty years as anything but the thing that could not be gone round." The grey face did not alter. "I find that I am curious. I do not say that I am easy."
    50	
    51	Lira had stopped chewing.
    52	
    53	"You can stand," she said. "You stood an hour at a post because a healer said so, and never moved. There's days when that's all that anybody's got."
    54	
    55	Vastin inclined his head to her, by the width of a finger, like somebody handed a small coin which he means to keep.
    56	
    57	---
    58	
    59	Seln was on the brow when they came up to him at dusk, and he had neither hand raised.
    60	
    61	The ridge broke off there, as it had on the hundred and twenty-third evening, and under it the valley began. Cael had carried that place in his head for a hundred and eighty days. He found that he had it right, and that it was larger. It was a great shallow head of pale ground, scooped out of the hills as a bowl is scooped, and open toward the south; and the light went level across it and laid the shadow of every tussock a yard long.
    62	
    63	The old ruts went round its head, high up, with turf in them. A furlong below the ruts there was the fence.
    64	
    65	It ran away on either hand with the curve of the ground, a row of small dark uprights set a pace apart, like stitches along a hem. He let his eyes go along it, as he had once, and under his breastbone a thin held note began to ask at every one. He told it no. Something else in him had begun to ask as well, lower down, in the voice of a post that has been stood in its hole: *This far?* He told that no also, and looked at the middle.
    66	
    67	The thing was there.
    68	
    69	It was grey, and upright, and it had a top that was not level. He had stood on this brow with the dusk coming and had not been able to make it; Lira had said that there was sky in it, and would not guess. There was half an hour more of light this evening, and it lay behind them.
    70	
    71	"It's a bridge," said Lira.
    72	
    73	She had her hand over her eyes.
    74	
    75	"Two legs, and a back. That's the sky in it; you're looking through under the back. It goes over something that I can't see the bottom of." She was silent for a breath. "There's no river. There's not a cup of water in that whole dish. What's it a bridge over?"
    76	
    77	"The sink," said Teague.
    78	
    79	He had said it to himself as much as to her.
    80	
    81	"There's a salt road two days north of this, on the high side, that looks down into here from a long way off. I've had it from three carters and never believed one of them. They say that there's a hole in the middle of this ground where the country's water goes, and a span standing over the hole, sunk down in the dish so that you see only the top of it." He put his thumbs in his belt. "They call it that. The Sunken Span. I took it for a tale about a bridge with no river."
    82	
    83	Karis had her notebook out, and had written the three words before he had done.
    84	
    85	But Seln had not been looking at the middle. He had been looking north, along the fence, and he lifted his chin toward it now by the width of a finger.
    86	
    87	"There," he said. "That was not there before the snow."
    88	
    89	A dry ravine came down out of the hills on that side, deep, with rock for its walls; and for a hundred yards or so it ran along the outside of the iron, so near that there was only a strip of level ground between its lip and the stakes. Something had been built on that strip.
    90	
    91	There was a hut, low, of turf and stone. There was a fire. There was a long shape beside the hut that was a hide thrown over something and weighted with stones. And across the strip, at the end nearer to the brow, where it was narrowest, two timbers had been laid one above the other on legs, as high as a man's chest.
    92	
    93	There were people at the timbers.
    94	
    95	Cael counted them. He had seen their like on a rim once, and in a stepped alley under a wall, and each time they had been six. There were seven. They stood in a row behind the upper baulk, and every one of them had a hood up and a cloth across the face, and every hood was turned toward the brow.
    96	
    97	They had not run to that barricade. They had been standing at it.
    98	
