   100	
   101	"That iron's been in the ground years. Not one cold term."
   102	
   103	It was Seln, and he had said it without turning from the fence.
   104	
   105	"I told you on this turf that it was a cordon, and that they had been here more than once. I had forty bars by one lamp when I said it. There is light enough tonight to see a quarter of the round." His voice was as mild as ever. "Some of that iron is bright, and a good deal of it is not; and a path has been trodden along the outside of it, the whole way, by people who walk it. Nobody wore that to the stone since the thaw, to meet twelve strangers. It was there for whoever came."
   106	
   107	"Two hundred and six," said Karis, very low. "From the first sheet."
   108	
   109	Down on the strip, a point of white light stood up over the timbers, and hung there, and went out.
   110	
   111	"That's one with a Path," said Teague. "He's telling me that he's got it." He had not taken his eyes from the place. "Two with bows, on the top baulk; you can see the stocks. Four with staves. And him."
   112	
   113	He turned round, and looked at the eleven of them, and at a mule.
   114	
   115	"Back, behind the brow. There'll be no fire."
   116	
   117	---
   118	
   119	They lay up in a fold a furlong behind the crest, with the mule's nose in a bag, and ate what there was cold.
   120	
   121	Teague did not sit. He had gone along the brow with Seln and Cael while the light held, bent double, to a place above the ravine's head where the whole strip lay under them like a thing on a table; they had been there a quarter of an hour, on their faces, and Seln had named what he saw in a murmur, piece by piece. Now a captain stood in the dusk with his thumbs in his belt, and looked down at the boy who had lain at his left.
   122	
   123	"Drawn?"
   124	
   125	It was one word, and Cael understood that he was being asked for a sum.
   126	
   127	He took out his knife. There was a patch of bare earth at the fold's bottom where sheep had lain once, and he smoothed it with the flat of his hand, and cut two lines down it, a hand apart, wavering a little.
   128	
   129	"That's the ravine. It's three men deep where it runs beside the fence, and the walls are sheer; I'd not want to go down them without a rope." He set a row of pricks along the right-hand side of it, close together. "That's the iron. And between the two there's a shelf. It's a hundred yards long, by my eye, and a cart wide for most of that. The line's a stride inside the iron, on the far side from the shelf, as it was the last time; Lira stopped a pace above the bars, and said that she'd go no farther."
   130	
   131	"I did," said Lira.
   132	
   133	"They've built on the shelf, since it's the only level ground on that side, and it's got a wall behind it that nobody can climb." He laid the knife's point at the nearer end. "Here it's at its narrowest. A cart would scrape. That's where the timbers are, and that's where I'd have put them. Two bows want a rest, and they want a short front, and they want anybody who's coming at them to come one behind another."
   134	
   135	He drew a square for the hut, thirty yards on, and a ring for the fire.
   136	
   137	"Behind the timbers it widens: five strides, perhaps. That's where they live. The hide's against the hut, on the ravine's side."
   138	
   139	"And behind that?"
   140	
   141	"It closes again."
   142	
   143	He had seen it last, lying on his face, and had not known what he was looking at until he asked himself what seven people on a posting would want; and then he had seen that a person who shuts himself onto a shelf wants a way off it.
   144	
   145	"The ravine bends there. It turns in toward the middle, and cuts the fence; you can see the bars stop on its near lip and begin again on the far one. At the bend its edge comes to within a stride and a half of the iron." He made the two lines nearly touch. "That's their door. Past it there's the path that Seln saw, a pace wide, going on round the fence to the east. It's the only way off that shelf that isn't through their own timbers."
   146	
   147	Teague looked at the square of earth for some time.
   148	
   149	"That's the ground," he said. "Now what's on it? She's to be asked, and I'm asking."
   150	
   151	Karis had come to the edge of the patch, with the hide under her arm. She did not unroll it.
   152	
   153	"It is the fourth ring. It has the stroke and the bar, as the three behind us have, and the office has held a sheet upon it for two hundred and six years. It is twice their measure: four hundred and forty yards from that iron to the middle, and two strides." She spoke as she would have read out a label. "And I have not one sheet from inside it. Nobody among us has put a foot over that line. I can tell you what three such places have done to five people, three times. They took every sigil at a stride, and gave it back at a stride, whole. I cannot tell you that a line of twice the round does the same."
   154	
   155	"She asked that, last time," said Brom. "The girl. Nobody could answer her."
   156	
   157	"Nobody can now. I should want one person to go a stride in, and a stride out, with a healer's fingers on the wrist, before I let anybody stand in it."
   158	
   159	"I'll be at the iron for that with my sleeves up," said Oryn, from where she sat by the mule. "On a morning when nobody's shooting across it."
   160	
   161	"Nobody's going into it tomorrow," said Teague. "I've a shelf to clear, and it's on this side."
   162	
   163	He was looking at the earth again; and Cael, who had drawn it, found that there was a thing that he had to say before a man with a wrist in leather began to say how.
   164	
   165	He had not planned it. He had been set at a shoulder to say what he saw, and the field was not his; he had said so in front of fifty people. But he had seen those hoods on a rim, with a light at the end of his own arm, and again in a cut, where he had only stood. He had seen what a captain's whole arm did to a ton of oak. And he had watched seven shapes stand at a baulk in the cold, with nowhere behind them but a gap a stride and a half wide.
   166	
   167	He gave it as a term, and he put his own word on the end of it, as a person sets a thumb to a slip.
   168	
   169	[[TIER-B: "Nobody declares. Nobody dies. They're seven people on a posting. Noted?" — the plan gives the four sentences whole and pins only the answer (Teague's first "Noted"). Staged as Cael's, said standing over the drawn earth to Teague, before any order for the morning is given; the paragraph above is the lead into it and the line below is Teague's pinned reply. If the unit is Teague's own and the reply another's, the two paragraphs above and the reply's tag want turning — coordinator to supply protected text]]
