   171	"Noted," said Teague.
   172	
   173	It was said level, and at once, and he did not seem to hear himself say it. But Lira's head had come round, and so had Brom's; and across the fold a boy with a cloth in his hands had stopped with it half wrung.
   174	
   175	"That's three of them now," said Brom, to nobody.
   176	
   177	Teague gave his orders after that, and they were few. They would go down at first light, when the sun would be behind them and in seven pairs of eyes. The quiet one was to go round the head of the dish that night, by the old ruts, and lie up beyond the far gap, where he could see what went out by it and what came in. The big one would walk in front, since a bolt had to be taken on something. The two healers and the beast were to stay at the ruts, a furlong up.
   178	
   179	"And you're at my left," he said to Cael. "With a stick. I'll want three breaths at those timbers with nothing coming over the top of them on that side. That's all of it."
   180	
   181	"And after the three?"
   182	
   183	"I'll say when I've seen it." The left hand had gone to the leather on the right wrist, and stayed there. "I've the one thing, and I can put it in front of that once."
   184	
   185	---
   186	
   187	Seln went round the head of the bowl in the dark, by the old ruts, with nothing in his hands.
   188	
   189	He had left the stave with the boy. It was a watchman's, shortened for a town, with a knife-cut a third of the way through its shaft. He had carried it from a stepped cut for thirteen days without once having a use for it, since he was not a person who fought with a stick. He had put it into Cael's hands behind the brow and said only that it would do for a shelf. The cord with the lead on its end was round his own waist, where it lived.
   190	
   191	There was no moon yet. It would rise late and thin, and he had counted upon that. He went at the pace of a man crossing his own yard, since haste makes a shape and a walk does not. When the ruts had carried him a third of the way round, he left them, and went down the slope on his heels until he could smell the iron.
   192	
   193	He had been fifteen years in a service that kept things. He had learned in it that a kept thing has its age written on it, if a person knows where to look. He did not touch the bars. He went along the outside of the fence on his knees, at the length of his arm, and put the backs of his fingers near each in turn, as a cook tries a pan.
   194	
   195	There were three ages of iron in that fence.
   196	
   197	He had not been able to say so by one lamp, a hundred and eighty days ago. He could say it now with his eyes shut. The most of the bars were scaled as the old stake at the Stair was scaled, in leaves, like bark. He would have put five cold terms upon those, and perhaps ten. Here and there among them stood a bar with only a skin of rust on it, set since. Where one had rotted at the turf, somebody had drawn it, and driven a new one into the same hole. And every thirty or forty paces he came to one of another kind. It was thicker than its neighbours by a finger, and its head had been beaten by a heavier mallet. The scale on it had scaled again, and come away, and scaled, until the thing was as rough as a root.
   198	
   199	He stopped a long while at the first of those.
   200	
   201	He could not put a figure on it. He had never been shown iron that had stood so long. He knew only that a man had come to this hillside with a mallet, a great many cold terms before the one who drove the rest, and had set a bar every thirty paces. People had come after him, time and again, to set the ones between.
   202	
   203	Under its beaten head each of the three kinds carried the same two figures, and beneath the figures a stroke and a bar. He read those with his thumb held a hair off the metal, where the punch had raised a lip.
   204	
   205	*A cordon,* he had said. He had been right, and he had not known the half of what he was right about.
   206	
   207	The path was a pace outside the bars.
   208	
   209	He had seen it from the brow as a pale thread. Under his palm it was stone. Boots had gone along it until the turf was gone, and the shale beneath had been trodden to a groove a hand deep. No posting of one cold term had done that, or of five. He knew what such a path was. There was one like it round the inner wall of the house above Ostrand, where the night clerk walked with his lantern. That one had been two lifetimes in the wearing.
   210	
   211	He did not walk on it. He went beside it, a yard up the slope, where the grass would keep no print.
   212	
   213	There was a sheet for this. That was the thought which stayed with him as he went, and he could not put it by. Somewhere west of the Line a clerk had a form with seven lines on it, and a column for what each of the seven was paid, and a space at the foot for whoever signed. There would be a second sheet for their meal, and a third for the carriage of water in kegs from a fold a day off. He had copied such sheets by the hundred. He had never once wondered what any of them was a posting *to*, since the form did not ask it.
   214	
   215	The case lay against his breastbone under his coat, shut, where it had lain for a great many nights. He had looked at a certain house from a roof, seventeen days ago, with that weight on him, and had ruled nothing. He had said at a fire that he would keep it a while longer.
   216	
   217	He had not thought, when he said so, that he would next meet that house's hands on a hill, in hoods, at the end of a ridge.
   218	
   219	It altered nothing about the case. He marked that it did not, and went on.
   220	
   221	---
   222	
